(a) By default ring = true, so the two ends are neighbours. cell_at(i) returns now[(i + n) % n]: for i = -1 that is cell 16, and for i = n it is cell 0. (b) Replace the ring setting with a three-way choice:
@export_enum("ring", "dead", "copy") var ending := 0

func cell_at(i: int) -> int:
	if ending == 0:
		return now[(i + n) % n]
	if i < 0 or i >= n:
		if ending == 1:
			return 0
		return now[clampi(i, 0, n - 1)]
	return now[i]
(c) Only from the row after a live cell first sits at an end cell. Before that, every ending reads 0 beyond the ends: the ring reads the other end cell, which is dead; the copy copies a dead end cell; dead is 0. From one middle seed, the live region grows at most one cell per row on each side, so the ends are first reached in row 8. Differences can appear only from row 9, starting at the border and spreading inward at most one cell per row. This holds for rules that answer 0 to 000 (all four here do). A rule that answers 1 to 000 would bring dead cells to life everywhere and could differ sooner. One thing I worked out: for Rules 90 and 250 the copy ending gives exactly the ring floor. Their rows stay mirror-symmetric, so on the ring cell 16 always equals cell 0, which is exactly what the copy reads. (d) None. The rule is defined on a row without ends, and no machine has one, so every ending is the author's choice. Making it a setting shows the choice and lets you test it. You can run the same rule with each ending, see what the edge decides (here 2, 6, 3 and 4 cells, always at the border from row 9), and tell features of the rule from features of the edge. Built in, the choice is hidden and its effect looks like the rule's own.

(a) By default ring = true: the ends are neighbours. cell_at(i) returns now[(i + n) % n], so i = -1 reads cell 16 and i = 17 reads cell 0. (b) Replace the bool with a three-way setting:
@export_enum("ring", "dead", "copy") var ends := 0
func cell_at(i: int) -> int:
	if ends == 0:
		return now[(i + n) % n]
	if i < 0:
		return 0 if ends == 1 else now[0]
	if i >= n:
		return 0 if ends == 1 else now[n - 1]
	return now[i]
(c) The ending is only consulted by cells 0 and 16, and what it returns is 0 under every ending until an end cell is alive. A cell reads only its neighbours, so life spreads at most one cell per row. From the middle seed, nothing can stand at an end before row 8, so only rows 9 and 10 (of 11) can differ. A prediction I then checked by simulating: for Rules 90 and 250 the copy ending gives exactly the ring's floor. Their rows stay symmetric, so cell 0 always equals cell 16, and 'copy the end' returns the same value as 'wrap round'. Rule 30 differed on 1 cell (row 10) and Rule 110 on 5 (rows 9 and 10). (d) None. The rule is defined on a row without ends, and every ending is the builder's choice. Making it a setting puts that choice in plain sight instead of hiding it in code where it looks like part of the rule. You can switch it, compare, and measure how much the floor depends on it (the lesson found 2, 6, 3 and 4 cells), and pick the ending your room needs.

(a) By default it is a ring: cell 0's left neighbour is cell 16, and cell 16's right is cell 0. The lookup adds n before taking % n: (i - 1 + n) % n turns -1 into 16, and (i + 1) % n turns 17 into 0. Adding n first keeps the number from going negative. (b)
@export_enum("Ring", "Dead end", "Copy") var ending: int = 0

func cell_at(i: int) -> int:
	if ending == 0:
		return now[(i + n) % n]
	if i >= 0 and i < n:
		return now[i]
	if ending == 1:
		return 0
	return now[clampi(i, 0, n - 1)]
This replaces `ring`, and step() reads its three neighbours through cell_at. (c) Rows 0 to 8 are identical under every ending. The first row that can differ is row 9, and only from there on. The rule looks one cell to each side, so the pattern grows at most one cell per row. Starting from cell 8 it needs 8 rows to reach cells 0 and 16. Endings disagree only in a lookup beyond an end, and only when that lookup returns a different value. Once a difference appears, it spreads inward one cell per row. For Rule 90 from a centred seed, copy never differs from ring at all: the floor stays mirror-symmetric, so cell 0 always equals cell 16, and 'copy of my own end' and 'the other end' give the same bit. Copy does differ from dead end from row 9. For an asymmetric rule like 30 it can differ from ring, but only once the two end cells disagree. (d) None. The rule is defined on a row without ends, and no machine has one. Every ending is a stand-in that agrees with the endless row until the pattern reaches the border. Making it a setting puts the choice on the surface: you can name it, compare the floors, see exactly which border cells come from the ending and not from the rule, and say in the README what you chose. It is no longer a hidden decision that passes for part of the rule.

(a) Each frame it adds delta (the seconds since the last frame) to clock. When clock reaches pace, it subtracts pace, makes one step and lays one row. Keeping the remainder means time beyond pace is not thrown away, so on average rows arrive exactly every pace seconds whatever the frame rate. Resetting to zero would lose the overshoot every time, so each interval would really be pace plus up to one frame, and the stair would grow slower, by an amount that depends on the frame rate. (b)
func _process(delta: float) -> void:
	if pace <= 0.0 or made >= rows:
		return
	clock += delta
	while clock >= pace and made < rows:
		clock -= pace
		step()
		lay_row()
lay_row() counts made up, so the made < rows guard stops at the stair's length even after a long stall. (c) The step reads only the previous row and the rule. Time (delta) never enters it, so the clock decides when each row is made, never what it is. The same sequence of steps gives the same rows. Pacing adds the experience of the making: you watch the history grow row by row in front of you, the walker meets rows as they arrive, and time in the room matches time in the rule. It also spreads the work over frames, so _ready() does not freeze while it lays many rows.

(a) By default (ring = true) the row is a ring: beyond cell 0 is cell 16, and beyond 16 is 0. The lookup wraps the index: (i - 1 + n) % n, (i + 1) % n, or now[(i + n) % n] in cell_at. The + n keeps -1 from staying negative before %.
(b) @export_range(0, 2) var ending := 0   # 0 ring, 1 dead end, 2 copy of the end cell
func cell_at(i: int) -> int:
	if ending == 0:
		return now[(i + n) % n]
	if i < 0:
		if ending == 2:
			return now[0]
		return 0
	if i >= n:
		if ending == 2:
			return now[n - 1]
		return 0
	return now[i]
This replaces ring, and step() reads cell_at(i-1)*4 + cell_at(i)*2 + cell_at(i+1). (c) The endings give different answers only for i = -1 and i = n, so only cells 0 and 16 can differ, and copy vs ring only when cell 0 != cell 16. From one middle seed the live region grows at most one cell per side per row, so an end cell can't be live before row 8. Rows 0 to 8 are identical, and differences can start in row 9 at the border, then spread inward a cell per row. For Rule 90 the floor stays symmetric, so cell 0 always equals cell 16 and copy gives exactly the ring floor; it differs from dead end at row 9. For 30 or 110, rows 9 and 10 can differ. (d) None. The rule is defined on a row without ends, and every ending stands in for something no machine has. Making it a setting shows it as a choice, lets you run the endings side by side to see which features come from the rule and which from the border, and stops a built-in choice passing as the rule.

(a) By default ring = true: the two ends are neighbours. Cell 0's left is cell 16, and cell 16's right is cell 0. cell_at returns now[(i + n) % n], so i = -1 becomes n - 1 and i = n becomes 0.
(b)
```
@export_enum("ring", "dead", "copy") var ending := 0   # replaces @export var ring

func cell_at(i: int) -> int:
	if ending == 0:
		return now[(i + n) % n]
	if i < 0 or i >= n:
		if ending == 1:
			return 0
		return now[clampi(i, 0, n - 1)]   # copy of the end cell
	return now[i]
```
(c) Endings differ only in what an end cell reads past the edge. Under every ending that reading is 0 while both end cells are dead. From one middle seed, life spreads at most one cell per row, so cells 0 and 16 cannot be alive before row 8. Row 9 is therefore the first row that can differ, and only at the border cells. After that a difference can creep inward one cell per row. For Rule 90 from this symmetric start, my working says copy and ring never differ: the floor is symmetric, so cell 0 always equals cell 16, and 'the other end' and 'my own end' are the same value. The 'from row 9' argument also needs 000 -> 0 (an even rule number). A rule with 000 -> 1 fills the row at once and reaches the ends by row 1.
(d) None of them. The rule's row is endless, and every ending is a decision about what a machine's finite row does. Making it a setting makes the choice visible and testable: you can switch it and see exactly which cells it changes (the border, from row 9). You can then tell a difference caused by the ending apart from one caused by the rule, instead of carrying a hidden decision you might mistake for the rule's own.

(a) By default the row is a ring: the two ends are neighbours. cell_at(i) returns now[(i + n) % n], so i = -1 gives cell 16 and i = 17 gives cell 0. Adding n first keeps the remainder from going negative for i = -1.
(b) The setting becomes a choice of three, using an @export_range as the lesson does:
```
@export_range(0, 2) var ending := 0   # 0: ring; 1: beyond an end is dead; 2: beyond an end copies the end cell

func cell_at(i: int) -> int:
	if ending == 0:
		return now[(i + n) % n]
	if i < 0:
		if ending == 2:
			return now[0]
		return 0
	if i >= n:
		if ending == 2:
			return now[n - 1]
		return 0
	return now[i]
```
(Remove the old ring line, and check the scene has no saved ring value you expect to matter.)
(c) The ending only enters through cell_at(-1) and cell_at(n). Past the left end the ring reads cell 16 where copy reads cell 0, and past the right end the ring reads cell 0 where copy reads cell 16. The two can disagree only in a row where cell 0 and cell 16 differ. From the middle seed, live cells spread at most one cell per row each way, so they first reach the ends at row 8. Rows 0 to 8 must be identical, and only row 9 onward (rows 9 and 10 of the eleven) can differ, starting at the border. More: for Rule 90 and Rule 250 every row is its own mirror, so cell 0 always equals cell 16 and the copy floor equals the ring floor on every row. Only lopsided rules such as 30 and 110 can show a difference, from row 9.
(d) None of them belongs to the rule. The rule speaks of a left and a right on an endless row, and no machine has one, so any ending is the maker's choice. Making it a setting puts the choice in the open and gives it a name, instead of hiding it in a %. You can also switch it and measure what the ending contributes apart from what the rule contributes (in the lesson, 2 to 6 cells, from row 9, at the border), and the record shows a decision someone made.

(a) By default ring is true: the two ends are neighbours, so the row is a ring. cell_at(i) returns now[(i + n) % n]. Adding n first keeps the remainder from going negative, so i = -1 reads cell n-1 and i = n reads cell 0.
(b) Replace the ring line with a three-way choice:
@export var ending := "ring"   # "ring": the ends are neighbours; "dead": beyond an end is 0; "copy": beyond an end repeats the end cell

func cell_at(i: int) -> int:
	if ending == "ring":
		return now[(i + n) % n]
	if i < 0:
		if ending == "copy":
			return now[0]
		return 0
	if i >= n:
		if ending == "copy":
			return now[n - 1]
		return 0
	return now[i]
(c) Only from the row after a live cell first reaches an end cell. A change spreads at most one cell per row. Until the pattern reaches cell 0 or cell 16, the end cells are 0 and the cells outside them are 0 under every ending, so all endings read the same values. With the seed at cell 8 of 17 the earliest is row 8, so differences can appear only in rows 9 and 10, which matches the lesson's 'always from row 9'. This assumes the rule leaves 000 dead, which holds for the even rule numbers used here; an odd rule would wake the ends at once. One more thing I worked out: for 90 and 250 'copy' gives exactly the ring's floor. Their rows stay mirror-symmetric, so cell 0 always equals cell 16, and reading your own end is the same as reading the far end. Only 30 and 110 can differ.
(d) None of them. The rule's row is endless, and no machine's row is, so every ending is the builder's decision. Making it a setting puts that decision in view and gives it a name. You can switch it and measure how much the floor depends on it (2, 6, 3 and 4 cells for the four rules), instead of a hidden choice passing for part of the rule.
